# The machines that run translate tasks: id, address, capacity.
#
# THIS FILE IS AN EXAMPLE AND IS NOT READ. Copy it once:
#
#     cp conf/nodes.conf.example conf/nodes.conf
#
# and edit the copy. bin/bt-cluster reads conf/nodes.conf, writes the
# Resource Manager's policy from it, and bin/oodt rewrites that policy at
# every start -- so a node list, once written, survives an upgrade.
#
# It is an example rather than the file itself because it used to be the
# file itself. Unpacking a release over a deployment put "local localhost 8"
# back over a two machine node list, the policy was then regenerated from
# that, and the run went on without the GPU node while reporting nothing
# wrong: half the throughput, no error, no mention.
#
# One line is a single machine install and needs no cluster at all.

# The machines this deployment may send translate work to.
#
# One node per line:  <id>  <host>  [capacity]
#
#   id        what the Resource Manager calls it. Any name; it appears in
#             nodes.xml, in the queue mapping, and in a task's ProcessingNode.
#   host      hostname or address reachable FROM THE MANAGER MACHINE. Not
#             localhost for a remote node, and not a name only that machine
#             can resolve: it is written into task metadata and read on the
#             node, where "localhost" means the node itself. That mistake cost
#             an afternoon -- see mnemosyne#320.
#   capacity  concurrent tasks. Defaults to 8.
#
# The first line takes one more optional field:
#
#   <id>  <host>  [capacity]  [conditions-capacity]  [managers-capacity]
#
#   conditions-capacity   concurrent condition evaluations on the manager.
#                         Defaults to 20.
#   managers-capacity     concurrent extract/join tasks on the manager.
#                         Defaults to 4, because the work is one extract and
#                         one join.
#
# conditions and managers each get a node id of their own on the first
# machine, not just a queue. The Resource Manager tracks load per node id, so
# a queue sharing an id shares its capacity: the join once sat queued behind
# 131 translate jobs on a full node after every translation had succeeded.
#
# Conditions are dispatched as tasks, and the Resource Manager tracks load per
# node id rather than per queue, so one id means one pool shared by every queue
# that node serves. On a shared pool a few long running translations hold every
# slot and the conditions gating the rest of the run cannot get one, so the
# gates never open and the queue drains a chunk at a time. bt-cluster therefore
# writes the manager out twice: once for translate and managers at [capacity],
# and once as "<id>-conditions" at [conditions-capacity], serving conditions
# alone.
#
# Size it for how many gates you expect to be open at once, not for the
# machine: a condition is a file read that finishes in milliseconds, while a
# translation holds its slot for minutes. It should be saturated and turning
# over constantly; the translate pool should be saturated and slow.
#
# Every machine uses the SAME absolute path for BIGTRANSLATE_HOME. The File
# Manager catalogues absolute paths and the Workflow Manager writes them into
# task metadata, so a node has to find bin, conf and the chunks exactly where
# the manager says they are -- which is why a node's tree is not under its own
# home directory. bt-cluster deploy puts it in the right place.
#
# The first line is the machine running the managers. It always serves both
# queues; every other node serves translate only, because extract and join
# read the corpus and write the Solr index and those disks are attached here.
#
# bin/bt-cluster reads this file. Nothing else needs editing to add a node:
#   bin/bt-cluster policy   regenerates nodes.xml and the queue mapping
#   bin/bt-cluster deploy   installs BigTranslate on each remote node
#   bin/bt-cluster stage    copies the chunks and conf each node needs
#   bin/bt-cluster start    brings up a batch stub and translator on each

local  localhost  8
